%YAML 1.1
%TAG !u! tag:unity3d.com,2011:
--- !u!114 &11400000
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 8de559a088a5d4d48bb00ae8ccc21165, type: 3}
  m_Name: Industrial Disaster
  crisisName: Industrial Disaster
  resolutionSplash: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 2f13bdb9a36278942a56f9ae4de7a587, type: 3}
  crisisStartSplash: {fileID: 21300000, guid: e18d4211cd2b541439191bf44a0f0374, type: 3}
  obviousWarningSplash: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 702b4abfbef267c4ba4fa0c79bed97b4, type: 3}
  ominousWarningSplash: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 256899730d1a58143a4ee02f659e2c49, type: 3}
  ominousWarningText: "The machinery groans under increasing strain. Engineers report irregular pressure in boilers, dangerous heat in furnaces, unstable supports in mines, and equipment failing well ahead of its expected service life. Production continues because factories and extraction sites cannot easily afford to stop, but accidents once dismissed as isolated mistakes are occurring with troubling frequency. Workers complain that maintenance crews are being asked to repair problems faster than they can understand them. Supervisors insist that output must be maintained, while engineers warn that temporary repairs are hiding deeper failures. The warning signs can be seen everywhere by those willing to look. Something inside the industrial system is coming apart."
  obviousWarningText: "The engineers have ordered emergency shutdowns and evacuations at several major industrial sites. Boilers are overheating faster than crews can cool them, mine supports are shifting under loads they once carried safely, and machinery is failing in clusters rather than one piece at a time. Production managers who resisted interruptions are now abandoning entire facilities rather than risk a catastrophic chain reaction. Workers pour out through factory gates while repair teams struggle to identify which systems can still be trusted. Mines, mills, and foundries that support the wider economy are beginning to fall silent. The industrial network is approaching a breaking point. If failures continue at this rate, the damage will spread far beyond a handful of factories."
  crisisStartText: "Years of strain have finally caught up with our industrial system. Mines, workshops, factories, and heavy machinery across the civilization are suffering serious failures, forcing shutdowns and disrupting the flow of materials that keeps the economy moving. Industrial buildings may become temporarily disabled or less productive, while mines and quarries struggle to maintain normal output. Repair crews can restore damaged facilities, but every worker and resource committed to recovery is one unavailable for expansion elsewhere. Production will suffer until the worst failures are brought under control. An industrial civilization has discovered the danger of depending upon machines that cannot be allowed to stop."
  resolutionText: "The failures have been brought under control. Mines, factories, and foundries that shut down in the crisis's worst days are relighting their furnaces and returning, carefully, to full production. Repair crews who worked without rest through the worst failures are recognized for preventing a far greater catastrophe, even as the cost of their labor strains the treasury. Engineers implement new safeguards, aware that the strain which caused the crisis could easily return if old habits resume unchecked. Workers return to mills and workshops that stood silent for weeks, grateful simply that the machinery held. The industrial system has survived its breaking point. Whether the lessons it taught will outlast the memory of the crisis remains to be seen."
  ominousWarningTurns: 1
  obviousWarningTurns: 1
  durationTurns: 7
  useAgeWindow: 1
  minimumAge: 12
  maximumAge: 16
  mechanic: 5
